# CHANGELOG.md is APPEND-ONLY: every PR adds its own `### ...` entry under the shared
# `## Unreleased` anchor, so a conflict here is two PRs both appending, never two PRs
# disagreeing about the same line. Union keeps both sides instead of stopping the merge.
#
# GITHUB DOES NOT HONOUR THIS. It affects LOCAL merges only. Measured 2026-08-08 on this
# repo, after the attribute was on main: 0 of 19 CONFLICTING PRs flipped to MERGEABLE,
# across TWO settled trigger-and-read cycles (GitHub computes mergeability lazily, so one
# unchanged reading is indistinguishable from a stale one and was not trusted alone). The
# real server-side merge agrees with the flag: `gh pr update-branch` refused cp#332 AND
# cp#350, 2 of 2, both of which merge CLEAN locally under this attribute. Positive control,
# same token and session: the same command SUCCEEDED on cp#353 and advanced its branch, so
# the refusals are the server's answer and not a broken tool.
#
# WHY IT IS HERE ANYWAY. It is the drain mechanism for the legacy queue. Emptying those PRs
# means `git fetch && git merge origin/main` in a clone and a push, and with this committed
# that costs ZERO hand-resolution of CHANGELOG.md for whoever does it. Left to personal git
# config it would have to be re-derived by every person who drains, and the one who does not
# know hand-resolves the same conflict once per PR.
#
# NOT THE REAL FIX. cp#358's changelog.d/ fragments are, and they cover every NEW PR; this
# covers only the backlog authored before them.
#
# REMOVE THIS ONCE THE LEGACY QUEUE IS DRAINED. After that nothing should be touching
# CHANGELOG.md directly, so the attribute is pure hazard: it would silently change local
# merge behaviour for no remaining reason.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
